    "chunk_text": "After listening to her at length, I advised her to go for physiotherapy\u2014a term she later admitted she didn\u2019t know. I felt sorry for her because she had three young children (a girl and two boys) and seemed helpless. Even though she appeared older than I, she was pretty, pleasant and kind.\n\nI was confused, and called the lady in Sri Lanka. She explained that the actual business process was for her to collect money from each worker applying for overseas employment, send half of that to the overseas agent and have the workers pay their own airfare. I told her that I did not like that scheme and advised her to stop that business. But she needed an income until she found some other line of work. She was a university graduate. I agreed to help her\u2014I was single, had plenty of money and also held a high-paying job. Gradually, our relationship turned into a romance.\n\nWhile working in Saudi Arabia, I would visit Sri Lanka most weekends. Also, I had bought a nice house in a pleasant neighborhood where I could stay when I visited. One morning, while in Sri Lanka, a Sub-inspector (SI) of Police visited my house looking for my friend. I discussed the reason for his visit and was told there were complaints of fraud against my friend from some prospective workers who had paid her money but did not get overseas employment. I took the SI for lunch and asked him to settle the cases with the workers saying I would meet the necessary cost.\n\nI thought that the problem had ended there. I was wrong. Months later I visited Singapore with my friend for a holiday. There, she had contacted a South Indian acquaintance who invited us for dinner. He did not take us to his apartment in a working class high-rise but seated us at a table in a food court attached to his building and ordered several bottles of beer\u2014large and warm, and food. I warned my friend not to drink as I suspected the intentions of the man."
  },
