    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0ba29cce78c8e8ed5f604f851dcb077e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1569,
    "chunk_text": "Gotabaya\u2019s candidacy for 2020 presidential election\n\nWe were now on the verge of a Presidential election to be held in 2020. The SLFP which I represented did not contemplate contesting this election since our leader Maithripala Sirisena did not show any inclination to come forward. The April bombing of churches and hotels had left his reputation in tatters. His behaviour in respect of this incident was unacceptable. It was rumoured that he was on a visit to India and Singapore connected to a family matter though a medical check up may also have been thrown in.\n\nThe near certainty of a terrorist attack was clearly intimated to the local security apparatus by Indian intelligence services but no action was taken. The toll of deaths and injuries were large and recalled memories of the internecine war which rocked the nation 10 years before. Our promising tourist industry suffered a severe setback. The Catholic Church led by Cardinal Malcolm Ranjith was up in arms. On the other hand the UNP led by Ranil was facing a major split. Sajith Premadasa led a large number of MPs out of the party to form the Samagi Jana Balawegaya [SJB].\n\nPremadasa became the likely right wing candidate to contest the forthcoming Presidential election though Ranil was prevaricating without giving him the nomination. At this stage Sirisena inducted me as the Chairman of the National Security Council which was the apex body in charge of coordinating intelligence regarding national security. Ranil, an ex-officio member, had stopped attending and we were very much on our own."
  },
