    "md5": "b6579b9ebeb3a87d26ec40af17c380e7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2006,
    "chunk_text": "Basil seated me with the leaders of other parties affiliated to GR\u2019s campaign. This may have woken up the SLFP Central Committee because leaders like Nimal Siripala also demanded the freedom to join the GR campaign. Some others wanted to \u201cwait and see\u201d the outcome of the conflict that was brewing between Ranil and Sajith. I however threw myself into GR\u2019s campaign during the last few months remaining in my Parliamentary tenure.\n\nHe also decided to discontinue the practice of accepting \u201cunsolicited projects\u201dwhich was the main reason for corruption in previous regimes. It had given an unfair advantage to MR\u2019s relatives and friends. He then ordered that his administration should desist from expensive \u201cshows\u201d and ceremonies and to get down to business. He himself began surprise visits to trouble spots as he had successfully done as Secretary of Defence and later as the Czar of Urban Development. None of this was pleasing to the Rajapaksa family who had decided that GR should be surrounded by MR loyalists headed by PB Jayasundera who had worked efficiently with Presidents from CBK\u2019s time.\n\nNot satisfied with this intervention through PB they later inducted Basil Rajapaksa, who was a dual citizen, as Finance Minister. Thus Basil, PB Jayasundera and Cabraal formed a steel ring round GR to continue the old MR policies which were universally recognized as having bred high level corruption. At this stage GR began to confide in his friends that he was increasingly isolated and unable to pursue the radical policies he had promised to the electorate. He had become a prisoner of the Rajapaksa family.\n\nThen a series of misfortunes and policy blunders hit the GR administration with disastrous results. Some were beyond his control while others were \u201cman made\u201d due to his lack of experience. The net result was that he had to leave office prematurely under humiliating conditions. It was a disaster of the highest magnitude and previously not seen in Sri Lankan politics."
  },
