    "language": "en",
    "md5": "1a813de3a7bd0cf7ae7e2cba507c1bc4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1948,
    "chunk_text": "In Sri Lanka, instead of pumping money from the Treasury to ailing enterprises PBJ presented a novel idea. To short circuit bureaucratic delays which the private sector had always complained about [particularly about delays in VAT reimbursements and organized corruption in delaying payments] he slashed taxes from the big companies which meant that government revenue was greatly reduced with all its attendant consequences. To do this he roped in WD Lakshman, his nominee to head the Central Bank, who allowed the printing of money to cover part of the deficit in accordance with new monetary theories emanating from US academia.\n\nWhat did happen was that these windfall savings were utilized by the new rich for consumption which tended to increase the foreign exchange deficit. Money was spent on latest automobiles, foreign travel and \u2018show\u2019 consumer items. Some of the richest salted away their windfall savings in foreign locations. The list of investors in secret funds like those divulged in Pandora and Panama papers and similar \u201cslush funds\u201d, is an indication of the corruption that marked the UNP regime.\n\nThis approach continued thanks to policy blunders of the GR presidency. An obvious result of this financial skulduggery was rapidly rising inflation which led to social distress, business disadvantages, rupee devaluation and added commitments by way of ISB (International Sovereign Bond) borrowings. PBJ\u2019s experiments had gone horribly wrong and laid the foundation for disaster when the unanticipated Covid epidemic hit the country. Covid emasculated all the sources of our national income such as tourism, foreign remittances, external trade and domestic production. Further foolish policy decisions-particularly about agriculture were in the pipe line, and the country was heading for an unprecedented disaster and GR was led to an ignominious exit which laid him and his party prostrate, perhaps never to rise again."
  },
