    "md5": "080590c9d08119b860134506efd0adf8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1776,
    "chunk_text": "What impressed me most was not merely the technical excellence of their work but the genuine enjoyment they derived from the entire process. The presentation itself was a masterpiece of storytelling.\n\nEven after forty-six years, I can still recall almost every detail of that presentation. It was impossible not to admire the pride these workers took in solving problems that affected their own workplace. They were not simply carrying out instructions from management; they were thinking, analysing, creating and improving. They were fully engaged.\n\nAs I watched them, I remember saying to myself, \u201cThis is something we must introduce in Sri Lanka.\u201d\n\nAnother memorable Quality Circle that we observed was in a completely different setting. Instead of a manufacturing environment, this one operated in a data-entry department. Six young women who entered data into computers faced a persistent problem. They frequently struggled to decipher the handwriting on the documents they received, resulting in costly errors.\n\nRather than accepting the situation as inevitable, they analysed the problem systematically. They measured the frequency of errors, identified which departments generated the problematic forms and categorised the types of handwriting mistakes that caused the greatest difficulties. Their investigations eventually led them to develop a standard manual prescribing how numerals and letters should be written on official forms. Management accepted the proposal and implemented it throughout the organisation. The result was a dramatic reduction in data-entry errors.\n\nThe lesson was unmistakable. Quality Circles were not confined to factories. They could improve any type of work where people were encouraged to think, analyse and solve problems together."
