    "chunk_text": "The following day, when I went to school, Miss Seetha saw my slate and praised me for doing a lot of homework. She ticked the bottom of the slate with chalk to mark that I had got my numbers right. I kept that tick until I went home that afternoon. My mother told me that Miss Seetha was very impressed with my knowledge and homework. Proud of me, my mother told me to study as my father had done. I, too, was proud of myself and decided to count to 100 soon and read the books my father had in his study.\n\nI was impatient to tell Miss Seetha that a few days later, I could count to 50. She kissed my forehead and stroked my head, saying, \u201cYou are a bright boy.\u201d\n\nIt was my duty to tell the captain about any suspicious movements at sea. If we shouted or wanted to go back to the house, the captain considered us mutineers and punished us with a cane. When he released us from our naval duties, Gamini and I played our own games. Our favorite was a ride in an improvised bus \u2013 an old wooden box placed on four empty milk powder. One of us pushed the box and the other \u2018drove\u2019. When the box fell off the tins, the pusher had to retrieve and replace them under the box.\n\nOne day I saw a nurse walking past our house. She smiled and waved at me and the next day I ran to the gate when I saw her coming from the hospital. She smiled, asked my name and introduced herself as Nilani Aunty. After that, on school days, Nilani Aunty stopped at our gate to chat with me.\n\nShe inquired about my studies, sports engagements, parents, and friends. Sometimes she brought me a few toffees or biscuits. She told me that she was a nurse at the hospital. I told her my mother was recently ill and had received some medicine from the hospital. She told me that my mother should tell her if she needed any help at the hospital."
