    "chunk_text": "Then she left me and started walking away. I watched her slow movement. She walked without looking back. I remember watching her sorrowfully as she disappeared at the far end of the road. I had not told my mother about Nilani Aunty. But when grief overwhelmed me that evening, I told her about her gentleness and how sad I would be to miss her.\n\nBut my sadness was unbearable when I thought about Nilani Aunty. My first loss. I wondered what would happen to me if my mother left me or died one day. My loneliness and sadness doubled. So I was determined to love only my mother as long as I lived. I have never met or heard from Nilani Aunty again. For several months, I had the urge to go to the gate and check whether she was back at the hospital.\n\nFirst confession and first Holy Communion are two major milestones in a Catholic child\u2019s spiritual growth. When I was six, I wanted to enroll in the parish priest\u2019s catechism classes. By then, I was a junior altar server who assisted at the Eucharist on Sundays. The parish priest allowed me to attend catechism classes.\n\nLengthy catechism classes preceded first confession and communion. At the end of the period of preparation, I wanted to confess and receive holy communion. The priest told me to wait at least one more year before receiving the two sacraments because I was too young. I insisted that I should be allowed to take part in the two events. I thought I could become holy through confession and the Eucharist.\n\nI blamed my mother for not supporting me. In the end, the priest reluctantly agreed to hear my confession and include me in the list of children eligible to receive Holy Communion. Each eligible boy and girl dressed in white as a bridegroom or bride for the occasion. I remember my father teasing me with his friends about my confessions. They wanted to know what sins I had committed as a young brat!"
