    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fb2f46f7dc68bd142e76b0048e217118",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1752,
    "chunk_text": "I believe the answer is clear. Sri Lanka must move beyond the narrow discussion of labour immigration and develop a comprehensive National Talent and International Mobility Strategy that supports national development while safeguarding national interests.\n\nLabour shortages are no longer isolated problems affecting a few industries. They have become a structural constraint on economic growth.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s demographic trends further reinforce this reality. Declining birth rates, an ageing population and continued emigration of skilled professionals mean that domestic labour supply alone may not be sufficient to meet future economic needs.\n\nMany countries facing similar circumstances have adopted proactive strategies to attract international talent. Singapore, Malaysia, South Korea, Australia, the United Arab Emirates and several European countries actively compete to attract skilled professionals, researchers, entrepreneurs and innovators. They recognise that talent has become one of the world\u2019s most valuable economic resources.\n\nSri Lanka cannot afford to remain outside this global competition.\n\nPublic discussion in Sri Lanka often centres on whether foreign workers should be allowed into the country. This debate is understandable but incomplete. The real question is much broader.\n\nWhat kind of people does Sri Lanka need to attract to achieve its national development goals?\n\n*\u00a0 Specialists capable of transferring knowledge and mentoring local workers.\n\nLabour immigration is, therefore, only one component of a much broader national strategy for talent attraction and international mobility.\n\nWhile discussing international talent, Sri Lanka must also recognise one of its greatest strengths, the global Sri Lankan diaspora."
  },
