    "chunk_text": "*\u00a0 Introduce specialised visa categories for investors, researchers and highly skilled professionals.\n\nThese measures demonstrate that attracting international talent is not incompatible with protecting national interests. On the contrary, effective governance strengthens both.\n\nMore recently, in May 2026, the Cabinet appointed a committee to formulate policy recommendations on labour immigration.\n\nHowever, Sri Lanka now has an opportunity to broaden this work beyond labour immigration and develop a forward-looking national strategy that addresses talent attraction, international mobility and diaspora engagement together.\n\nSri Lanka requires a long-term strategy built on several complementary pillars.\n\nFirst, a national system should forecast future workforce requirements by sector, occupation and skills level so that policy decisions are guided by evidence rather than short-term pressures.\n\nSecond, immigration policies should facilitate the recruitment of highly skilled professionals where genuine shortages exist while protecting opportunities for Sri Lankan workers through transparent labour market testing.\n\nThird, specialised visa pathways should be introduced for investors, entrepreneurs, researchers, academics, digital professionals and internationally recognised experts.\n\nFourth, a National Diaspora Skills Bank should connect the expertise of Sri Lankans living abroad with national priorities in research, education, innovation, entrepreneurship and public administration.\n\nFifth, Sri Lanka should position itself as an attractive destination for international students, researchers and knowledge partnerships, recognising that educational mobility often leads to long-term economic and innovation benefits.\n\nFinally, modern digital systems, including biometric identity verification, integrated visa processing, qualification verification and secure information sharing should underpin all aspects of talent mobility."
  },
