    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka\u2019s geographical proximity to India presents a unique opportunity that few countries enjoy. India has emerged as one of the world\u2019s leading sources of highly skilled professionals, entrepreneurs, researchers, technology specialists and innovators. As India\u2019s economy continues to expand and bilateral economic cooperation deepens, opportunities for the movement of professionals, researchers, investors and knowledge workers between the two countries are likely to increase.\n\nIn the 21st century, countries will increasingly compete not only for investment but also for talent, knowledge and innovation. Sri Lanka\u2019s future prosperity will depend on its ability to connect with the world\u2019s best minds while mobilising the expertise of its own global diaspora. As one of the world\u2019s fastest-growing knowledge economies and Sri Lanka\u2019s closest neighbour, India represents a natural strategic partner in this journey. By developing a comprehensive National Talent and International Mobility Strategy today, Sri Lanka will be better positioned to harness these opportunities, strengthen regional cooperation and build a more competitive, innovative and resilient economy for the future.\n\nIf Sri Lanka is to become a competitive upper-middle-income economy, it must aspire not only to export talent to the world but also to attract the world\u2019s talent to Sri Lanka.\n\nShantha Kulasekara is the Executive Director of the Institute of Migration Sri Lanka (IMSL), an independent, non-profit institution dedicated to advancing migration governance, international mobility and diaspora engagement. With over 30 years of national and international experience, he previously served with the International Organisation for Migration (IOM), the UN Migration Agency, as Head of Migration Governance in Sri Lanka and Head of Office in the Maldives. The views expressed are those of the author. He can be reached at: hantha@instituteofmigration.lk"
