    "md5": "1f876c6c80f7760220b6800b7ec73450",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1666,
    "chunk_text": "The night that went wrong \u2026\n\nYes, it was supposed to be waves of sound, but it ended up being waves of frustration.\n\nThe headliners took the stage, as promised, and the crowd showed up, but they were thoroughly disappointed.\n\nBy mid-evening the complaints were louder than the music. Too many people, too little space to move. Food and drinks? A marathon, not a sip. Some fans, with valid tickets, couldn\u2019t even get past the entry point.\n\nOverseas, and out-of-town supporters, flew, or drove in\u2026 only to spend the evening uncomfortable, boxed in, and disappointed.\n\nThe seating arrangement was a complete mess, with people standing throughout the event, and the only way many guests could see the stage was by climbing onto chairs.\n\nMore importantly, what if there had been an emergency or a fire?\n\nThen came the last verse no one wrote: a personal clash in the crowd. Security stepped in. The show ended early \u2026 abruptly.\n\nAccording to the stars of the show, the night was meant to be memorable for music, not for mayhem.\n\nThey\u2019re reviewing everything, and promising that any future shows, under their banner, will hit the standard fans actually deserve \u2014 comfort, safety, organisation, the whole package.\n\nFans are already debating it. Some say \u201cApology accepted, we\u2019ll be back.\u201d\n\nOthers, who were greatly inconvenienced, say: \u201cWe need to think twice before attending such events!\u201d\n\nThe CPC\u2019s decisive role in China\u2019s rise to economic superpower\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
